# Shell scripts must keep LF endings or the shebang breaks on Linux, which is exactly
# where a reviewer is most likely to run them from a Windows-authored checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf
# The Makefile too. GNU make treats a trailing carriage return as part of the command, so a
# CRLF checkout fails with `python: command not found` and a confusing trailing quote.
Makefile text eol=lf
# Packaging and dependency files, so a Windows checkout cannot produce a different install
# than a Linux one.
*.toml text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.cfg text eol=lf

# Committed evidence is compared byte-for-byte by run_submission.sh, so it must not be
# rewritten by autocrlf on checkout.
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
# Generated markdown too: `make report`, `make attention` and `make benchmarks` write LF,
# so checking these out as CRLF would leave a fresh clone permanently dirty after the
# README's own instructions were followed.
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.png binary
*.parquet binary
*.xz binary
